## The `infra-telemetry.service.j2` file defines **how Linux should run and manage your telemetry collector**. When Ansible deploys this template, it creates a systemd service that runs `collector.py` using the project's Python virtual environment. `WorkingDirectory` tells the program where it lives, while `TELEMETRY_DB_PATH` tells it where to store the SQLite database. `After=network.target` makes sure basic networking is available before the collector starts. `Restart=always` and `RestartSec=5` make the service resilient: if the collector crashes, systemd waits five seconds and starts it again. Finally, `WantedBy=multi-user.target` allows the service to be enabled during normal Linux boot, so the collector can automatically start after a reboot. In short, **Ansible installs the service definition, systemd manages the collector's lifecycle, and the Python collector continuously gathers and stores infrastructure telemetry.**
[Unit]
Description=Infrastructure Telemetry Collector
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=simple
ExecStart=/opt/infra-telemetry/venv/bin/python /opt/infra-telemetry/collector.py
WorkingDirectory=/opt/infra-telemetry
Environment=TELEMETRY_DB_PATH=/opt/infra-telemetry/data/telemetry.db
Restart=always
RestartSec=5

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
